{"seqbook":1,"address":"00.00.00"}
{"n":1,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0000.00.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"---\naddress: 00.00.00.0000\ntitle: kernel\nstate: draft\ntype: system\n---\n\n## Registry\nformat: 7\n\nR10 (DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md:40): face 00 is the registry, the place that lists the page's faces. Layout is `7` (not given).\n\n- `00.draft` registry: draft because registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0000"}
{"n":2,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0051.00.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"---\naddress: 00.00.00.0051\ntitle: seqaddr\nstate: draft\ntype: system\n---\n\n## Registry\nformat: 7\n\nR10 (DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md:40): face 00 is the registry, the place that lists the page's faces. Layout is `7` (not given).\n\n- `00.draft` registry: draft because registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `01.draft` law: draft because its cards are named in open conflicts (CONFLICTS K05, K06, K07, K11; QUESTIONS Q13, Q14). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `02.draft` schema: draft because its cards are named in open conflicts (CONFLICTS K05, K06, K07, K11; QUESTIONS Q13, Q14). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `04.draft` user: draft because holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `06.01.draft` notes: draft because this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0051"}
{"n":3,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0051.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"its cards are named in open conflicts (CONFLICTS K05, K06, K07, K11; QUESTIONS Q13, Q14). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Full Address\n\nA page or face always has a full address `DD.SS.BB.PPPP[.FF[.NN]]`. Short form is a way of *writing* it that leaves out ancestors. Those are inferred from the active book's book, shelf and desk.\n\n## Page Anchor\n\nThe page is the only 4-digit axis. Any address with a 4-digit group can be read by position alone:\n\n```\n[DD.][SS.][BB.] PPPP [.FF][.NN] [.tail]\n```\n\n- Right of the page: 1 group = `FF`, 2 groups = `FF.NN`.\n- Left of the page, reading right to left: `BB`, then `SS`, then `DD`.\n- The number of groups on the left decides what is inferred:\n\n| Written | Inferred from active book | Example in `10.11.21` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PPPP...` | DD, SS, BB | `4442.01` -> `10.11.21.4442.01` |\n| `BB.PPPP...` | DD, SS | `12.2523.04` -> `10.11.12.2523.04` |\n| `SS.BB.PPPP...` | DD | `30.12.2523` -> `10.30.12.2523` |\n| `DD.SS.BB.PPPP...` | nothing | already full |\n\n## Container Address\n\nAn address with no 4-digit group is always read from the desk: `21` is desk 21, `10.11` is a shelf, `10.11.21` is a book. To refer to a sibling book in short form, use its sentinel page: `21.0000` is book 21 on the active shelf.\n\n## Counters\n\n`NN` exists only directly after `FF`. Counters are not an axis, so the reserved 01-09 range does not apply to them. The first counter is `01`.\n\n## Exact Widths\n\nEvery group must have its exact width. `4442.1` is invalid, not padded to `4442.01`.\n\n## Active Page\n\nThe session has one active book, and usually an active page, but sometimes several pages are in play. A bare `04` cannot mean \"face 04 of the active page\", because A1.3 already reads it as desk 04.\n\nA **leading dot** means \"relative to the active page\".\n- `.04` is face 04 of the active page.\n- `.05.03` is task 03 on the active page.\n- No other address starts with a dot, so this never collides with A1.3.\n- If more than one page is active, `.04` is ambiguous and is elevated to the user (same as A1.8).\n\n## Short Form\n\nUsers may write short form. Models always write the full address. When a user writes short form, the model states the expanded full address back. If the address was inherited (A1.8), the model also states where it was found.\n\n## Finding\n\n- **Reading** (is it well formed?) uses only A1.2-A1.5. It needs no context beyond the active book.\n- **Finding** (where is it?) looks up in order: book -> shelf -> desk -> kernel. The first match wins. This matters for the inherited reserved pages (0020-0049).\n- If nothing is found, the address is **elevated to the user** to resolve. It is never guessed.\n\n## Live Inheritance\n\nBooks follow the kernel template live; they do not copy it at creation. Anything a book does not override is found through the A1.8 lookup (book -> shelf -> desk -> kernel), so a kernel change reaches every book that has not overridden it.\n\n## Title Position\n\nA word after DD, SS, BB or PPPP is a title. A word after FF or NN is a tail if it is a tail word, otherwise a card key (amended by C8.2). Faces never have titles.\n\n## Faces Carry No Title\n\nA face is written `PPPP.FF` or `PPPP.FF.NN`. `2423.05.01` is page 2423, face 05 (tasks), counter 01: one task. \"Go to 2423.05.01 and run it\" is unambiguous.\n\n## Title Characters\n\nLowercase letters, digits and hyphens. Starts with a letter. No dots, no spaces.\n\n## Titles Are Labels\n\nA title is not part of identity: `4442` and `4442.plan` are the same page. Several pages may share a title on purpose. This is how a band set works (A2.6).\n\n## Which Pages Need A Title\n\n1. Pages 1000 and up must have a title, stored in the stamp.\n2. Pages 0001-0999 may have a title; their number is already their name. Page 0000 has none (it holds the book's title in its stamp).\n3. A title in a written address is optional. If written, it is checked against the stamp. A mismatch (`4442.timer` when the stamp says `clock`) is elevated to the user.\n4. Tail words can never be titles (A2.5).\n\n## Door Page\n\nEach desk, shelf and book has an door page. Its stamp holds the container's title.\n\n| Container | Identity page |\n|---|---|\n| Book `10.11.21` | `10.11.21.0000` |\n| Shelf `10.11` | `10.11.00.0000` |\n| Desk `10` | `10.00.00.0000` |\n\n## Checked Labels\n\nA title written in a container address (`10.11.21.tools`) is checked against the door page's stamp. A mismatch is elevated to the user (A2.8).\n\n## Door Rule\n\nOne rule for every container axis:\n\n| Address | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `DD.00.00` | the desk's own book (door, law, overrides) |\n| `DD.00.BB` | a desk book: owned by the desk, no shelf |\n| `DD.SS.00` | the shelf's own book |\n| `DD.SS.BB.0000` | the book's own page |\n\nConsequences:\n- The A1.8 lookup uses real books: book `10.11.21`, then shelf `10.11.00`, then desk `10.00.00`, then kernel. An inherited page such as `0020` lives at `10.11.00.0020` or `10.00.00.0020`.\n- A container's state uses page tails (A2.5): `10.00.00.0000.retired` retires the desk.\n- Shelf 00 and book 00 are never ordinary shelves or books.\n\n## Missing Is Valid\n\nAbsence carries meaning and is not an error: no `9999` means the book does not continue.\n\n## Pointer\n\nAt every level, 99 is a pointer, never content. It lists all valid paths onward. Its face 08 (map) records each target and the kind of link (continuation or child).\n\n| Where | Pointer to |\n|---|---|\n| `DD.99` (door `DD.99.00.0000`) | child desks |\n| `DD.SS.99` | shelf continuations or children |\n| `....9999` | book continuations |\n| `PP99` | band continuations |\n\nMissing means no continuation (A4.6).\n\n## Many Targets\n\nA 99 pointer may list several targets. One desk can have several child desks, and one book can continue in several places.\n\n## Back Links\n\nEvery target's door page must link back to the pointer that names it. A link that only goes one way is a validation error.\n\nChild desks take their own top-level desk number (10-98). Hierarchy beyond the fixed depth exists only through these links.\n\n## Derived Ancestors\n\nEach pointer's **direct** links (A5.2, A5.3) are the source of truth. An ancestor's list of all locations further down the chain is **derived**: regenerated by walking the direct links whenever a continuation is added, never edited by hand. This meets the requirement while keeping one source of truth, so a missed or partial update can always be repaired by regenerating.\n\n## Overflow\n\n**Bands.** When a band has no free page, it continues: `PP99` points to the next free hundred, which becomes the continuation band. Example: band `1000` is full and the first free hundred is `2100`, so `1099` points to `2100`.\n\nBands never grow in place; they always continue through `PP99`. This keeps A2.6 intact: fixed 100-page bands, every `xx00` a head, and sets linked by number. Items in a continuation band (for example `2123`) do not line up by number with their set in other bands, so they link to their set explicitly in face 08.\n\n## Namespace\n\nEach repository is its own address space. Desk `10` in one repository is unrelated to desk `10` in another.\n\nEvery repository contains its own kernel (desk 00). Kernel branches (desks 01-09) are downloaded into the repository as needed.\n\n## Kernel And Branches\n\n| Desk | Use |\n|---|---|\n| `00` | kernel. `00.00.00` is its own book, `00.00.00.0000` its door page. The A1.8 lookup ends here. |\n| `01-09` | kernel branches |\n| `10-98` | user environments. An instantiated workspace is a desk 10 or above. |\n\nExample branch: SoF (Science of Fiction), a space for building a book using RPG elements to test paths and branches, rewind the story and fork it. Likely a shelf on desk 01. A fork maps naturally to a multi-target 99 pointer (A5.2).\n\n## Bands\n\nA page number reads as `NDNN`. `D` (the second digit) is the band.\n\n```\n0 4 23\nN D NN     D = 4 = dev band, NN = 23 = item in the band\n```\n\n- **Fixed width.** Every band is exactly 100 pages, `PP00` to `PP99`.\n- **Head by number.** Every page at or above 0100 ending in `00` is a band head. It holds the band's definition and contents. No marker or tail is needed: listing pages that end in `00` finds every band.\n- **`PP99` is continuation.** A band that outgrows 100 pages points to its continuation from `PP99`, as page 9999 does for a book. This mirrors the axis sentinels: `00` start, `99` end.\n- **Sets across bands are linked by number.** Pages with the same `N` and `NN` in different bands are one set: `0423.timer` (dev) and `0523.timer` (build). Titles should match; a mismatch is elevated to the user (A2.8).\n\n## Book Layout\n\nThe kernel is the template for `0000-0999` in every book, including what each band digit means there.\n\n| Range | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `0000` | book door (sentinel) |\n| `0001-0099` | reserved; a page here is a local override of the inherited environment |\n| `0100-0999` | standard bands modelled by the kernel (`0100` ... `0900`) |\n| `1000-9998` | user space; users design their own bands (`1000` ... `9800`) |\n| `9999` | continuation (sentinel) |\n\n`1000` is the head of the first user band, which is why the first open page is `1001`.","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0051"}
{"n":4,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0051.02.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"its cards are named in open conflicts (CONFLICTS K05, K06, K07, K11; QUESTIONS Q13, Q14). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Grammar\nsource: docs/spec/seqAddr.md:264-284\n\n```\naddress    = container / page-addr / active-rel\ncontainer  = desk [ \".\" shelf [ \".\" book ] ] [ \".\" title ]\npage-addr  = [ ancestors \".\" ] page [ \".\" page-end ]\nancestors  = book / shelf \".\" book / desk \".\" shelf \".\" book\npage-end   = face-part / title [ \".\" page-tail ] / page-tail\nactive-rel = \".\" face-part\nface-part  = face [ \".\" counter ] [ \".\" tail ] [ \".\" card [ \".\" brand ] ]\n\ndesk       = 2DIGIT\nshelf      = 2DIGIT\nbook       = 2DIGIT\nface       = 2DIGIT\ncounter    = 2DIGIT                       ; 01-98\npage       = 4DIGIT\nbrand      = 3DIGIT                       ; 001-998\ncard       = LOWER *( LOWER / DIGIT / \"-\" )   ; not a tail word\ntitle      = LOWER *( LOWER / DIGIT / \"-\" )   ; not a tail word\npage-tail  = \"draft\" / \"retired\"\ntail       = \"draft\" / \"retired\" / \"pack\" / \"open\" / \"return\" / \"review\"\n```","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0051"}
{"n":5,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0051.04.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Full Address\nsource: [01 / Full Address] decisions.md:12; 04 text: seqAddr.md:14-24\nwhy: 4.7\n\nFive axes, highest on the left, separated by dots. Widths are exact: `4442.1` is invalid. [A1.5]\n\n| Axis | Width | Name | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `DD` | 2 | desk | highest controlling environment |\n| `SS` | 2 | shelf | themed work space |\n| `BB` | 2 | book | localized environment |\n| `PPPP` | 4 | page | the work inside a book |\n| `FF` | 2 | face | one view of a page |\n\nA counter `NN` (2 digits, 01-98) may follow a face. Counters are not an axis and attach only to faces. [A1.4]\n\n## Page Anchor\nsource: [01 / Page Anchor] decisions.md:15-30; 04 text: seqAddr.md:71-86; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:15\nwhy: The page is the only 4-digit axis.\n\nThe page is the only 4-digit axis, so any address containing it reads by position alone:\n\n```\n[DD.][SS.][BB.] PPPP [.FF][.NN] [.title][.tail]\n```\n\n- Right of the page: one group is `FF`, two are `FF.NN`.\n- Left of the page, reading right to left: `BB`, `SS`, `DD`.\n- Missing ancestors are taken from the active book.\n\n| Written | Inferred | In active book `10.11.21` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PPPP...` | DD, SS, BB | `4442.01` -> `10.11.21.4442.01` |\n| `BB.PPPP...` | DD, SS | `12.2523.04` -> `10.11.12.2523.04` |\n| `SS.BB.PPPP...` | DD | `30.12.2523` -> `10.30.12.2523` |\n| `DD.SS.BB.PPPP...` | nothing | full address |\n\n## Container Address\nsource: [01 / Container Address] decisions.md:33; 04 text: seqAddr.md:89\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`21` is desk 21, `10.11` is a shelf, `10.11.21` is a book. A sibling book in short form is written through its door page: `21.0000`.\n\n## Counters\nsource: [01 / Counters] decisions.md:36; 04 text: seqAddr.md:24\nwhy: 4.7\n\nA counter `NN` (2 digits, 01-98) may follow a face. Counters are not an axis and attach only to faces. [A1.4]\n\n## Exact Widths\nsource: [01 / Exact Widths] decisions.md:39; 04 text: seqAddr.md:14\nwhy: 4.7\n\nFive axes, highest on the left, separated by dots. Widths are exact: `4442.1` is invalid. [A1.5]\n\n## Active Page\nsource: [01 / Active Page] decisions.md:42-48; 04 text: seqAddr.md:92; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:42\nwhy: A bare `04` cannot mean \"face 04 of the active page\", because A1.3 already reads it as desk 04.\n\n`.04` is face 04 of the active page, and `.05.03` is task 03 on it. If several pages are active, a leading-dot address is elevated to the user.\n\n## Short Form\nsource: [01 / Short Form] decisions.md:51; 04 text: seqAddr.md:95\nwhy: 4.7\n\nUsers may write short form. Models always write the full address and state it back when a user wrote short form, including where it was found if inherited.\n\n## Finding\nsource: [01 / Finding] decisions.md:54-56; 04 text: seqAddr.md:98-104\nwhy: 4.7\n\nReading needs only the rules above. Finding looks up in order:\n\n```\nbook 10.11.21 -> shelf 10.11.00 -> desk 10.00.00 -> kernel 00.00.00\n```\n\nThe first match wins. Books follow the kernel live: anything not overridden locally is found in the kernel. If nothing is found, the address is elevated to the user, never guessed.\n\n## Live Inheritance\nsource: [01 / Live Inheritance] decisions.md:128; 04 text: seqAddr.md:104; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:128\nwhy: so a kernel change reaches every book that has not overridden it.\n\nThe first match wins. Books follow the kernel live: anything not overridden locally is found in the kernel. If nothing is found, the address is elevated to the user, never guessed.\n\n## Title Position\nsource: [01 / Title Position] decisions.md:69; 04 text: seqAddr.md:110-111\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- A word after `DD`, `SS`, `BB` or `PPPP` is a title. Faces never have titles: a face is `PPPP.FF` or `PPPP.FF.NN`. [A2.1, A2.2]\n- A word after a face is a **card key**, not a title: `10.10.11.1014.01.ham` is card `ham` on face 01. A 3-digit group after a card is a brand number: `ham.002`. [C8.2]\n\n## Faces Carry No Title\nsource: [01 / Faces Carry No Title] decisions.md:72; 04 text: seqAddr.md:110\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- A word after `DD`, `SS`, `BB` or `PPPP` is a title. Faces never have titles: a face is `PPPP.FF` or `PPPP.FF.NN`. [A2.1, A2.2]\n\n## Title Characters\nsource: [01 / Title Characters] decisions.md:75; 04 text: seqAddr.md:112\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- Characters: lowercase letters, digits, hyphens. Starts with a letter. No dots or spaces. [A2.3]\n\n## Titles Are Labels\nsource: [01 / Titles Are Labels] decisions.md:78; 04 text: seqAddr.md:114; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:78\nwhy: Several pages may share a title on purpose. This is how a band set works (A2.6).\n\n- A title is a label, not identity. `4442` and `4442.timer` are the same page, and several pages may share a title. [A2.4]\n\n## Which Pages Need A Title\nsource: [01 / Which Pages Need A Title] decisions.md:131-134; 04 text: seqAddr.md:115-116\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- Pages 1000 and up must have a title. Pages 0001-0999 may. Page 0000 has none. [A2.8]\n- A title stored in a stamp is the truth. A title written in an address is checked against it, and a mismatch is elevated to the user. [A2.8, A3.2]\n\n## Door Page\nsource: [01 / Door Page] decisions.md:143-149; 04 text: seqAddr.md:56-62\nwhy: 4.7\n\nEvery container is identified by a page 0000, whose stamp holds its title. [A3.1]\n\n| Container | Identity page |\n|---|---|\n| Book `10.11.21` | `10.11.21.0000` |\n| Shelf `10.11` | `10.11.00.0000` |\n| Desk `10` | `10.00.00.0000` |\n\n## Checked Labels\nsource: [01 / Checked Labels] decisions.md:152; 04 text: seqAddr.md:116\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- A title stored in a stamp is the truth. A title written in an address is checked against it, and a mismatch is elevated to the user. [A2.8, A3.2]\n\n## Door Rule\nsource: [01 / Door Rule] decisions.md:161-173; 04 text: seqAddr.md:44-53\nwhy: 4.7\n\n00 at any level means \"the door\". [A4.1]\n\n| Address | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `DD.00.00` | the desk's own book |\n| `DD.00.BB` | a desk book: owned by the desk, no shelf |\n| `DD.SS.00` | the shelf's own book |\n| `DD.SS.BB.0000` | the book's own page |\n| `PPPP.00` | the page's own registry face |\n\n## Missing Is Valid\nsource: [01 / Missing Is Valid] decisions.md:205; 04 text: seqAddr.md:254; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:205\nwhy: Absence carries meaning and is not an error\n\n- Missing is a valid state: no `PP99` or `9999` means no continuation. [A4.6]\n\n## Pointer\nsource: [01 / Pointer] decisions.md:214-224; 04 text: seqAddr.md:249\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- 99 at any level is a pointer to the next location. It lists every valid path onward. Its face 08 records each target and the kind of link (continuation or child).\n\n## Many Targets\nsource: [01 / Many Targets] decisions.md:227; 04 text: seqAddr.md:250\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- A pointer may list several targets: several child desks, several continuations, or a fork.\n\n## Back Links\nsource: [01 / Back Links] decisions.md:230-232; 04 text: seqAddr.md:251, seqAddr.md:253\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- Every target's door page links back. A one-way link is invalid.\n\n- Child desks take their own desk number. Hierarchy beyond the fixed depth exists only through links.\n\n## Derived Ancestors\nsource: [01 / Derived Ancestors] decisions.md:237; 04 text: seqAddr.md:252; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:237\nwhy: This meets the requirement while keeping one source of truth, so a missed or partial update can always be repaired by regenerating.\n\n- Each pointer's direct links are the source of truth. An ancestor's list of everything further down the chain is derived by walking the links, never edited by hand.\n\n## Overflow\nsource: [01 / Overflow] decisions.md:246-250; 04 text: seqAddr.md:256-258; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:250\nwhy: This keeps A2.6 intact: fixed 100-page bands, every `xx00` a head, and sets linked by number.\n\n### 7.1 Overflow\n- **Bands:** a full band continues through `PP99` to the next free hundred (`1099` -> `2100`). Bands never grow in place. Items in a continuation band link to their set explicitly in face 08.\n\n## Namespace\nsource: [01 / Namespace] decisions.md:253, decisions.md:265; 04 text: seqAddr.md:41\nwhy: 4.7\n\nEach repository is its own namespace and contains its own kernel. [A6.2, A6.4]\n\n## Kernel And Branches\nsource: [01 / Kernel And Branches] decisions.md:256-262; 04 text: seqAddr.md:33-39\nwhy: 4.7\n\n### 1.3 Desks [A6.3]\n| Desk | Use |\n|---|---|\n| `00` | kernel. Its own book is `00.00.00`. |\n| `01-09` | kernel branches, downloaded into the repository as needed |\n| `10-98` | user environments |\n| `99` | reserved pointer |\n\n## Bands\nsource: [01 / Bands] decisions.md:102-112; 04 text: seqAddr.md:232-243; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:110\nwhy: No marker or tail is needed: listing pages that end in `00` finds every band.\n\n### 6.1 Bands [A2.6]\nA page number reads as `NDNN`. `D`, the second digit, is the band.\n\n```\n0 4 23\nN D NN     D = 4 = dev band, NN = 23 = item in the band\n```\n\n- Every band is exactly 100 pages, `PP00-PP99`.\n- Every page at or above `0100` ending in `00` is a band head, holding the band's definition and contents.\n- `PP99` is the band's continuation.\n- Pages with the same `N` and `NN` in different bands form a set: `0423.timer` (dev) and `0523.timer` (build). Titles should match.\n\n## Book Layout\nsource: [01 / Book Layout] decisions.md:115-125; 04 text: seqAddr.md:215-228\nwhy: 4.7\n\nThe kernel is the template for `0000-0999` in every book.\n\n| Range | Use |\n|---|---|\n| `0000` | book door |\n| `0001-0009` | special reserved pages |\n| `0010-0019` | contents, dictionary, concordance and other required pages |\n| `0020-0049` | localized behaviour: exists locally or is inherited |\n| `0050-0099` | structural pages, mostly in the kernel or templates |\n| `0100-0999` | standard bands modelled by the kernel |\n| `1000-9998` | user space; users design their own bands |\n| `9999` | book continuation |\n\nA page in `0001-0099` that exists in a book is a local override of the inherited environment.\n\n## Grammar\nsource: [02 / Grammar] seqAddr.md:264-284; 04 text: seqAddr.md:286-293\nwhy: 4.7\n\nRules the grammar does not express:\n- `retired` only without a counter; `pack`, `open`, `return`, `review` only with one.\n- Containers need their door page to be legal.\n- Pages 1000 and up need a title in their stamp.\n\nAxiom chains are not addresses. A chain lives only in an `axioms:` field; its grammar is in `Axioms.md` and decision E3. A one-digit mark is an axiom, never an axis: every address axis is at least two digits.","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0051"}
{"n":6,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0051.06.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Seed Session\n\nPrima seed session, 2026-11-11. Note fields are open (decisions.md C7), so this note carries none (`7`).\n\n- R9: counter overflow through `FF.99` struck from Overflow and Pointer. `7`: counter overflow after 98.\n- Open, copied (seqAddr.md section 9):\n\n- C1.4: whether continuation pages share the `pointer` type.\n- C4: how the compute holds and traces face 08 links.\n- C5: authority precedence between levels.\n- C7: notes metadata.\n- C8.7, C8.10: failures against assumed brands only; where a new brand card goes.\n- D: drafting mechanism (proposed in `drafting.md`), task failure paths, claims.\n- Proposals awaiting the Director that touch this document: a task and ticket split of face 05 counters, link kinds for face 08, and a concordance page (decision E9).\n- Merges from Director rulings (docs/spec/DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md: R1 door, R9 no face sentinels, R10 face 00 registry), one per change, copied text otherwise unchanged:\n  - R1 [04 / Container Address]: \"identity page\" -> \"door page\"\n  - R1 [01 / Door Page]: \"identity page\" -> \"door page\"\n  - R1 [01 / Checked Labels]: \"identity page\" -> \"door page\"\n  - R1 [01 / Door Rule]: \"(identity, law, overrides)\" -> \"(door, law, overrides)\"\n  - R10 [04 / Door Rule]: \"own identity face\" -> \"own registry face\"\n  - R9 [04 / Door Rule]: \"| `FF.00` | a counted face's board |\" -> \"\"\n  - R1 [04 / Door Rule]: \"the owner of the level above, itself\" -> \"the door\"\n  - R9 [01 / Missing Is Valid]: \"no `FF.00` means the face is not counted, no `FF.99` means no continuation,\" -> \"\"\n  - R9 [04 / Missing Is Valid]: \"no `FF.00` means not counted, no `FF.99`, `PP99` or `9999` means no continuation\" -> \"no `PP99` or `9999` means no continuation\"\n  - R9 [01 / Pointer]: \"| `FF.99` | counter continuations |\" -> \"\"\n  - R1 [01 / Pointer]: \"(identity `DD.99.00.0000`)\" -> \"(door `DD.99.00.0000`)\"\n  - R1 [01 / Back Links]: \"identity page\" -> \"door page\"\n  - R1 [04 / Back Links]: \"identity page\" -> \"door page\"\n  - R9 [01 / Overflow]: \"**Counters.** When `FF.98` exists, `FF.99` points to the same face on the **next free page in the same band**. Counting restarts at `01` there, and that page's `FF.00` board links back (A5.3). Old counters never move.\" -> \"\"\n  - R9 [04 / Overflow]: \"- **Counters:** once `FF.98` exists, `FF.99` points to the same face on the next free page in the same band. Counting restarts at `01`. Old counters never move.\" -> \"\"\n  - R1 [01 / Kernel And Branches]: \"identity page\" -> \"door page\"\n  - R1 [01 / Book Layout]: \"book identity (sentinel)\" -> \"book door (sentinel)\"\n  - R1 [04 / Book Layout]: \"| book identity |\" -> \"| book door |\"\n  - R9 [02 / Grammar]: \"; 00 board, 01-98, 99 continuation\" -> \"; 01-98\"\n  - R9 [04 / Grammar]: \"- A counter requires its face's `00` board.\" -> \"\"\n  - R9 [04 / Grammar]: \"- Counter `00` is the board itself; write it as `FF.00`.\" -> \"\"\n  - R1 [04 / Grammar]: \"identity page\" -> \"door page\"","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0051"}
{"n":7,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0052.00.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"---\naddress: 00.00.00.0052\ntitle: faces\nstate: draft\ntype: system\n---\n\n## Registry\nformat: 7\n\nR10 (DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md:40): face 00 is the registry, the place that lists the page's faces. Layout is `7` (not given).\n\n- `00.draft` registry: draft because registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `01.draft` law: draft because edited by R9 and R10 in this pass; the stamp form is open (Q8). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `02.draft` schema: draft because edited by R9 and R10 in this pass; the stamp form is open (Q8). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `04.draft` user: draft because holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `06.01.draft` notes: draft because this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0052"}
{"n":8,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0052.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"edited by R9 and R10 in this pass; the stamp form is open (Q8). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Registry Face\n\n`PPPP.00` holds the page's stamp: address, title, state. Faces carry no stamp of their own; the page stamp is stored once.\n\n## Face Ranges\n\n| Faces | Use |\n|---|---|\n| `00` | page registry (A4.2) |\n| `01-09` | system faces (01-08 defined, 07 reserved, 09 unassigned) |\n| `10-98` | not used in this design; reserved for later customization |\n| `99` | reserved |\n\n## Face Jobs\n\n| Face | Name | For |\n|---|---|---|\n| `00` | registry | the page stamp |\n| `01` | law | agents: the meaning of the page, in model-efficient cards |\n| `02` | schema | model or compute: templates, formats, exact processes |\n| `03` | operate | compute: operations for executing a card; the card menu; where authority comes from. Mainly in the kernel and desk books. |\n| `04` | user | users: the why of the page, long form, for inspection |\n| `05` | tasks | working agents: passes, as counters |\n| `06` | notes | models leaving information: one counter per session |\n| `07` | reserved | not active |\n| `08` | map | links: authority, dependencies, what is needed to find and use the page. With face 00, where the page's lineage is kept, by the compute [E6] |\n| `09` | reserved | unassigned |\n| `10-98` | reserved | for later customization |\n| `99` | reserved | |\n\n## Which Faces\n\nA page is valid when it has face 00 and at least one of faces 01-06 or 08. Face 07 is not active and does not count. A page with only a registry has no reason to exist.\n\n## Draft Faces\n\nA draft face (`04.draft`, `05.01.draft`) counts toward C1.1 and may exist on any page, live or draft. A brand-new page may start with only drafts.\n\n## Retiring\n\nIf the last content face is retired, the page is retired with it. The compute then updates every derived path that referred to it: contents lists, ancestor lists and incoming links (A5.4).\n\n## Cards\n\n- A card is a `##` heading in a face's Markdown.\n- Its key is the heading in title form: `Retry Policy` -> `retry-policy`.\n- Keys are unique within a face. Cards with the same key on different faces are the same card.\n- A face's contents list is derived from its cards, never written by hand.\n\n## Full Explanation\n\nEvery card on face 01 or 02 has a matching 04 card with the same key:\n\n```\n## Job\nsource: [01 / Job], [02 / Job]\nwhy: Routing should reduce reading without replacing the source.\n<full explanation, written for a person>\n```\n\nA card title (its `##` heading) is also stored in the dictionary (C8).\n\n## Hard Rules And Findings\n\n### C3.2 Hard rules and findings\n| Check | Kind |\n|---|---|\n| Every 01/02 card key has a 04 card | hard: the compute rejects the write |\n| That 04 card has a `why:` line and a body | hard |\n| The 04 body is not identical to the 01 body | finding: flagged for review |\n| The 01 card's keywords appear in the 04 card | finding |\n| A 01 card and its 04 card disagree | elevated to the user for correction; neither governs (axiom 1) |\n\n## Face 03\n\n- Face 03 is allowed in normal books on their reserved pages (0001-0099). The kernel and desk books may use it anywhere.\n- User pages are mostly face 04. A 04 section may have a 01 card: a brief of how a model should understand it. It has a 02 card only when the section describes a task process.\n\n## Counted Faces\n\n- A counted face is never seated. The seated address `DD.SS.BB.PPPP.FF` applies only to faces without counters.\n- Faces 05 (tasks) and 06 (notes) are always counted. Other faces may be counted on certain page types, for example 03 or 04 on a contents or dictionary page.\n\n## Packed Face\n\nStatus: settled. A face 01 card is the packed form of its 04 card. A model that needs more requests the why from the compute by card key. When the two disagree, it is elevated to the user (C3.2).\n\n## Lineage On The Page\n\n- Lineage lives with the page; its book, shelf and desk rule over it.\n- It appears on face 00 and face 08. It is not user-facing: the compute keeps it.\n- It is travelled in two directions, back and forward, through structure and through time.\n- An override links back to who authorized it.\n\n## Links Face\n\nFace 08 is the links face: every connection to other pages that matters to this page. Most pages have none; some have many. The compute holds all connections in memory, so it can see and trace them. How the compute stores and traces them is still to be designed.","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0052"}
{"n":9,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0052.02.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"edited by R9 and R10 in this pass; the stamp form is open (Q8). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Stamp\nsource: docs/spec/decisions.md:176,285; seqAddr.md:172; format.md:74\nform: ---\\ntitle: tools\\n---\n\n`PPPP.00` holds the page's stamp: address, title, state. Faces carry no stamp of their own; the page stamp is stored once.\n\n- **`type` is a field in the page stamp** (face 00).\n\n- Any card, and the page stamp, may carry an `axioms:` field: a chain of axioms attached to it (`axioms: 7.8`). A cite runs nothing and permits nothing. [E2]\n\n{\"n\":1,\"at\":\"2026-11-09T14:02:11Z\",\"by\":\"user\",\"op\":\"put\",\"addr\":\"10.11.21.0000.00\",\"body\":\"---\\ntitle: tools\\n---\"}\n\n## Card Shape\nsource: docs/spec/decisions.md:508-509; seqAddr.md:168-172\n\n- `type:` is a field line directly under the card heading. Field lines (`name: value`) come first, then a blank line, then the body.\n- Each face has a default type, so most cards need no `type:` line.\n\n- A card is a `##` heading. Its key is the heading in title form (`Retry Policy` -> `retry-policy`), unique within a face. The same key on two faces is the same card.\n- Field lines (`name: value`) follow the heading, then a blank line, then the body.\n- A face's contents list is derived from its cards.\n- Every card has a **card type**, given by a `type:` field or by the face's default (`rule` on 01, `schema` on 02, `menu` on 03, `why` on 04, `task` on 05, `note` on 06, `link` on 08). Card types are defined in the kernel.\n- Any card, and the page stamp, may carry an `axioms:` field: a chain of axioms attached to it (`axioms: 7.8`). A cite runs nothing and permits nothing. [E2]","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0052"}
{"n":10,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0052.04.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Registry Face\nsource: [01 / Registry Face] decisions.md:176; 04 text: seqAddr.md:64\nwhy: 4.7\n\nA page is identified by its face 00, which holds the page stamp (address, title, state). Faces carry no stamp of their own. [A4.2]\n\n## Face Ranges\nsource: [01 / Face Ranges] decisions.md:179-184\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## Face Jobs\nsource: [01 / Face Jobs] seqAddr.md:147-160; 04 text: kernel-design.md:94, kernel-design.md:96-107; why: docs/spec/kernel-design.md:94\nwhy: Different readers need different representations of the same subject.\n\nThe kernel should use the same SeqAddr page architecture that it governs rather than becoming a separate, incompatible documentation format. Different readers need different representations of the same subject.\n\n- **Face 00 - Registry:** page stamp and registry.\n- **Face 01 - Law:** compact model-facing understanding and governing meaning.\n- **Face 02 - Schema:** formats, templates, and precise procedures.\n- **Face 03 - Operate:** compute-oriented operations, routing/menu, and authority references, where allowed by SeqAddr.\n- **Face 04 - User:** readable explanation, design intent, and the preserved *why*.\n- **Face 05 - Tasks:** working passes where appropriate.\n- **Face 06 - Notes:** attributed observations and unresolved findings.\n- **Face 08 - Map:** lawful relationships, dependencies, and navigation.\n\nNot every page needs every face. The selected page type and its inherited contracts determine what is required. Readers should retrieve the relevant face and only the additional governing or connected information needed for the task.\n\nThe faces are different representations, **not equal sources of authority**. Conflicts should be identified and resolved by the governing agreements, not averaged into an invented compromise.\n\n## Which Faces\nsource: [01 / Which Faces] decisions.md:274; 04 text: seqAddr.md:163; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:274\nwhy: A page with only a registry has no reason to exist.\n\n- A page needs face 00 and at least one of faces 01-06 or 08. Face 07 does not count.\n\n## Draft Faces\nsource: [01 / Draft Faces] decisions.md:277; 04 text: seqAddr.md:164\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- Draft faces count and are allowed on any page. A new page may start with only drafts.\n\n## Retiring\nsource: [01 / Retiring] decisions.md:280; 04 text: seqAddr.md:165\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- Retiring the last content face retires the page, and the compute updates every derived path to it.\n\n## Cards\nsource: [01 / Cards] decisions.md:298-301; 04 text: seqAddr.md:168-170\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- A card is a `##` heading. Its key is the heading in title form (`Retry Policy` -> `retry-policy`), unique within a face. The same key on two faces is the same card.\n- Field lines (`name: value`) follow the heading, then a blank line, then the body.\n- A face's contents list is derived from its cards.\n\n## Full Explanation\nsource: [01 / Full Explanation] decisions.md:339-348; 04 text: seqAddr.md:175-186\nwhy: 4.7\n\nEvery 01 or 02 card has a 04 card with the same key, written for a person:\n\n```\n## Job\nsource: [01 / Job], [02 / Job]\nwhy: Routing should reduce reading without replacing the source.\n<full explanation>\n```\n\n- Hard (the compute rejects the write): the 04 card exists, and has a `why:` line and a body.\n- Findings (flagged for review): the 04 body copies the 01 body; the 01 card's keywords (its brands) are missing from the 04 card.\n- User pages are mostly 04. A 04 section may have a 01 card, a brief of how a model should understand it, and a 02 card only when it describes a task process. [C6]\n\n## Hard Rules And Findings\nsource: [01 / Hard Rules And Findings] decisions.md:350-357; 04 text: seqAddr.md:184-185\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- Hard (the compute rejects the write): the 04 card exists, and has a `why:` line and a body.\n- Findings (flagged for review): the 04 body copies the 01 body; the 01 card's keywords (its brands) are missing from the 04 card.\n\n## Face 03\nsource: [01 / Face 03] decisions.md:404-405; 04 text: seqAddr.md:189\nwhy: 4.7\n\nFace 03 is used mainly in the kernel and desk books. In a normal book it is allowed on the reserved pages (0001-0099), such as the dictionary.\n\n## Counted Faces\nsource: [01 / Counted Faces] decisions.md:198-199; 04 text: seqAddr.md:199-201\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- A counted face is never seated.\n- Faces 05 and 06 are always counted. Others may be counted on certain page types (for example 03 or 04 on a contents or dictionary page).\n- A face without counters is seated at `DD.SS.BB.PPPP.FF`. `FF.draft` is its unseated draft. `FF.retired` is a previously seated face awaiting removal.\n\n## Packed Face\nsource: [01 / Packed Face] decisions.md:575\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## Lineage On The Page\nsource: [01 / Lineage On The Page] decisions.md:579-582; 04 text: seqAddr.md:157; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:584\nwhy: authority without lineage is only hierarchy (Director).\n\n| `08` | map | links: authority, dependencies, what is needed to find and use the page. With face 00, where the page's lineage is kept, by the compute [E6] |\n\n## Links Face\nsource: [01 / Links Face] decisions.md:368; 04 text: seqAddr.md:157\nwhy: 4.7\n\n| `08` | map | links: authority, dependencies, what is needed to find and use the page. With face 00, where the page's lineage is kept, by the compute [E6] |\n\n## Stamp\nsource: [02 / Stamp] decisions.md:176, decisions.md:285, seqAddr.md:172, format.md:74; 04 text: kernel-design.md:96\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- **Face 00 - Registry:** page stamp and registry.\n\n## Card Shape\nsource: [02 / Card Shape] decisions.md:508-509, seqAddr.md:168-172\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0052"}
{"n":11,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0052.06.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Seed Session\n\nPrima seed session, 2026-11-11. Note fields are open (decisions.md C7), so this note carries none (`7`).\n\n- R9 (DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md:37): card Counter Continuation (decisions.md:202) removed, and Counted Faces now copies only decisions.md:198-199 / seqAddr.md:199-201 (lines on `FF.00` left out). `7`: what follows counter 98 is not designed now that `FF.99` is gone.\n- Open, copied: Face 08 is the links face: every connection to other pages that matters to this page. Most pages have none; some have many. The compute holds all connections in memory, so it can see and trace them. How the compute stores and traces them is still to be designed.\n- Open, copied: Open: what exactly `02`, `03` and `05-09` grant.\n- Open, copied: Status: open, to discuss.\n- Merges from Director rulings (docs/spec/DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md: R1 door, R9 no face sentinels, R10 face 00 registry), one per change, copied text otherwise unchanged:\n  - R10 [01 / Face Ranges]: \"| page identity (A4.2) |\" -> \"| page registry (A4.2) |\"\n  - R10 [01 / Face Jobs]: \"| `00` | identity |\" -> \"| `00` | registry |\"\n  - R10 [04 / Face Jobs]: \"**Face 00 - Identity:** page stamp and identity.\" -> \"**Face 00 - Registry:** page stamp and registry.\"\n  - R10 [01 / Which Faces]: \"A page with only an identity has\" -> \"A page with only a registry has\"\n  - R10 [04 / Which Faces]: \"A page with only an identity has\" -> \"A page with only a registry has\"\n  - R9 [01 / Retiring]: \"it: boards, contents lists\" -> \"it: contents lists\"\n  - R9 [01 / Counted Faces]: \"Plain `FF` on a counted face means `FF.00`.\" -> \"\"\n  - R9 [04 / Counted Faces]: \"Plain `FF` on a counted face means `FF.00`.\" -> \"\"\n  - R10 [04 / Stamp]: \"**Face 00 - Identity:** page stamp and identity.\" -> \"**Face 00 - Registry:** page stamp and registry.\"\n- R4 (DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md:15, plain characters only): replaced U+2014 x7 with ASCII (dashes to \"-\", section sign to \"section \", curly quotes to straight).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0052"}
{"n":12,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0053.00.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"---\naddress: 00.00.00.0053\ntitle: tails\nstate: draft\ntype: system\n---\n\n## Registry\nformat: 7\n\nR10 (DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md:40): face 00 is the registry, the place that lists the page's faces. Layout is `7` (not given).\n\n- `00.draft` registry: draft because registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `01.draft` law: draft because copies workflow-loop.003.md, which says \"state: draft - Director review required; not live\" (:3). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `02.draft` schema: draft because copies workflow-loop.003.md, which says \"state: draft - Director review required; not live\" (:3). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `04.draft` user: draft because holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `06.01.draft` notes: draft because this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0053"}
{"n":13,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0053.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"copies workflow-loop.003.md, which says \"state: draft - Director review required; not live\" (:3). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Where Tails Attach\n\nA tail follows a title or a face:\n- Page: `PPPP.title.tail`\n- Face: `PPPP.FF.tail`\n- Counter: `PPPP.FF.NN.tail`\n\nOnly a tail may follow a title.\n\nTail words are reserved and can never be titles. `PPPP.draft` and `PPPP.plan.draft` both mean page PPPP in draft.\n\n## Allowed Tails\n\nAllowed tails per level:\n\n| Level | Tails |\n|---|---|\n| Page | `draft`, `retired` |\n| Face | `draft`, `retired` |\n| Counter | `draft`, `pack`, `open`, `return`, `review` (bare = closed) |\n| Desk / shelf / book | none |\n\n## Workflow State\n\nWorkflow state lives only on counters. A container takes its state from its sentinel page: `10.11.21.0000.retired` retires the book, `10.11.21.0000.draft` is a book still being set up.\n\n## Tail Meanings\n\n| Tail | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `draft` | being written; not active |\n| `pack` | ready to run |\n| `open` | being run by a worker |\n| `return` | what was done |\n| `review` | under review |\n| `retired` | previously seated, retired before removal |\n\n## Closed Is Bare\n\n`closed` is the label for the **bare** `05.NN` counter. Its separately recorded `outcome` may be `passed`, `failed`, `blocked`, `needs-authority`, or `abandoned` **(approved S03)**.","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0053"}
{"n":14,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0053.02.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"copies workflow-loop.003.md, which says \"state: draft - Director review required; not live\" (:3). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Tail Moves\nsource: docs/spec/workflow-loop.003.md:162-164; format.md:124\n\n```\ndraft -> pack -> open -> return -> review -> closed\n```\n\n- the tail move is allowed (for example `pack -> open`, never `pack -> return`)","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0053"}
{"n":15,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0053.04.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Where Tails Attach\nsource: [01 / Where Tails Attach] decisions.md:81-88; 04 text: seqAddr.md:122\nwhy: 4.7\n\nA tail follows a title or a face. Only a tail may follow a title.\n\n## Allowed Tails\nsource: [01 / Allowed Tails] decisions.md:90-97; 04 text: seqAddr.md:124-129\nwhy: 4.7\n\n| Level | Written | Tails |\n|---|---|---|\n| Page | `PPPP.title.tail` or `PPPP.tail` | `draft`, `retired` |\n| Face | `PPPP.FF.tail` | `draft`, `retired` |\n| Counter | `PPPP.FF.NN.tail` | `draft`, `pack`, `open`, `return`, `review`; bare = closed |\n| Desk / shelf / book | none | state comes from the door page: `10.11.21.0000.retired` |\n\n## Workflow State\nsource: [01 / Workflow State] decisions.md:99; 04 text: seqAddr.md:140\nwhy: 4.7\n\nWorkflow state lives only on counters.\n\n## Tail Meanings\nsource: [01 / Tail Meanings] seqAddr.md:131-138\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## Closed Is Bare\nsource: [01 / Closed Is Bare] workflow-loop.003.md:166; 04 text: decisions.md:625-626,629 (F.1 S03); why: docs/spec/decisions.md:629\nwhy: The same lifecycle can preserve distinct reasons the work ended.\n\n| Decision | Approved direction and scope | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| S03 | Close at the bare counter and record the outcome separately, including passed, failed, blocked, needs-authority, and abandoned. | The same lifecycle can preserve distinct reasons the work ended. |\n\n## Tail Moves\nsource: [02 / Tail Moves] workflow-loop.003.md:162-164, format.md:124\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0053"}
{"n":16,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0053.06.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Seed Session\n\nPrima seed session, 2026-11-11. Note fields are open (decisions.md C7), so this note carries none (`7`).\n\n- Open, copied (drafting.md): - How review is recorded, and what happens when review rejects a draft (section D).\n- Merges from Director rulings (docs/spec/DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md: R1 door, R9 no face sentinels, R10 face 00 registry), one per change, copied text otherwise unchanged:\n  - R1 [04 / Allowed Tails]: \"identity page\" -> \"door page\"\n  - R9 [01 / Closed Is Bare]: \"`closed` is the board label for\" -> \"`closed` is the label for\"","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0053"}
{"n":17,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0054.00.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"---\naddress: 00.00.00.0054\ntitle: types\nstate: draft\ntype: system\n---\n\n## Registry\nformat: 7\n\nR10 (DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md:40): face 00 is the registry, the place that lists the page's faces. Layout is `7` (not given).\n\n- `00.draft` registry: draft because registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `01.draft` law: draft because C1.4 has an open question (decisions.md:292). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `02.draft` schema: draft because the card-type table is in open question Q11 (brand cards and 04). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `04.draft` user: draft because holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `06.01.draft` notes: draft because this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0054"}
{"n":18,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0054.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"C1.4 has an open question (decisions.md:292). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Page Types\n\nPage number alone is not enough to say what a page is for. A page type is a property that rules attach to.\n\n- **`type` is a field in the page stamp** (face 00).\n- **Starting types:** `system`, `general`, `code`, `workflow`, `band`, `pointer`.\n- **Defined in the kernel.** Each type is a card on a kernel structural page (0050-0099), marked `owner: kernel-only`, so no book can redefine it. Live inheritance applies.\n- **Rules a type can carry:** required or forbidden faces, page ranges it may live in, who may write it, whether drafting (D0) is required.\n- **Default:** the page stamp's type, else the default set by the page's band head, else `general`.\n- **Changing a type** goes through drafting and review like any other change.\n\n## Card Types\n\nA card type is to a card what a page type is to a page (C1.4).\n\n- `type:` is a field line directly under the card heading. Field lines (`name: value`) come first, then a blank line, then the body.\n- Each face has a default type, so most cards need no `type:` line.\n- Types are defined in the kernel on a structural page, `owner: kernel-only`, inherited live. A definition states: faces allowed, required and optional fields, fields only the compute writes, whether a 04 card is required, hard checks and findings, whether drafting applies.","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0054"}
{"n":19,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0054.02.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"the card-type table is in open question Q11 (brand cards and 04). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Card Type Table\nsource: docs/spec/decisions.md:512-522\n\n| Type | Default on | Fields | Needs a 04 card |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `rule` | 01 | `owner` | yes |\n| `schema` | 02 | `source`, `form` | yes |\n| `menu` | 03 | entries derived by the compute | no |\n| `why` | 04 | `source`, `why` | - |\n| `task` | 05 | section D | no |\n| `note` | 06 | C7 | no |\n| `link` | 08 | `target`, `kind` | no |\n| `brand` | 01 | `brand`, `forms`, `means`, `meters` (compute) | no |\n| `index` | 0012.03 | `default`, brand list | no |","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0054"}
{"n":20,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0054.04.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Page Types\nsource: [01 / Page Types] decisions.md:283-290; 04 text: seqAddr.md:192-194; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:283\nwhy: Page number alone is not enough to say what a page is for.\n\n- `type` is a field in the page stamp (face 00): `system`, `general`, `code`, `workflow`, `band`, `pointer`.\n- Defined in the kernel, `owner: kernel-only`, inherited live. A type carries rules: required or forbidden faces, allowed page ranges, who may write, whether drafting applies.\n- Default: the stamp's type, else the band head's default, else `general`. Changing a type goes through drafting and review.\n\n## Card Types\nsource: [01 / Card Types] decisions.md:506-510; 04 text: seqAddr.md:171\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- Every card has a **card type**, given by a `type:` field or by the face's default (`rule` on 01, `schema` on 02, `menu` on 03, `why` on 04, `task` on 05, `note` on 06, `link` on 08). Card types are defined in the kernel.\n\n## Card Type Table\nsource: [02 / Card Type Table] decisions.md:512-522\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0054"}
{"n":21,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0054.06.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Seed Session\n\nPrima seed session, 2026-11-11. Note fields are open (decisions.md C7), so this note carries none (`7`).\n\n- Open, copied: Open: `pointer` covers contents, dictionary and concordance pages. Do continuation pages (`99`, `9999`, `PP99`) share that type, or get their own?","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0054"}
{"n":22,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0055.00.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"---\naddress: 00.00.00.0055\ntitle: brands\nstate: draft\ntype: system\n---\n\n## Registry\nformat: 7\n\nR10 (DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md:40): face 00 is the registry, the place that lists the page's faces. Layout is `7` (not given).\n\n- `00.draft` registry: draft because registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `01.draft` law: draft because R9 struck rows from it in this pass; brand-card 04 rule is open (Q11). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `02.draft` schema: draft because R9 struck rows from it in this pass; brand-card 04 rule is open (Q11). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `04.draft` user: draft because holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `06.01.draft` notes: draft because this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0055"}
{"n":23,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0055.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"R9 struck rows from it in this pass; brand-card 04 rule is open (Q11). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Brands\n\nA word can carry several meanings. Each meaning is a **brand**, `word.NNN`, backed by a **brand card**. A user's explained meaning of a word is recorded as a brand, so models never fall back on an average meaning.\n\n## Card Key Address\n\nA word after a container is a title (A2.1); a word after a face is a card key. `10.10.11.1014.01.ham` is card `ham` on face 01 of page 1014. Tail words stay reserved, so they never collide with card keys. A 3-digit group can only be a brand number: `ham.002`.\n\n## Dictionary Is An Index\n\nThe dictionary (page 0012, face 03, counted) maps a word to its brand cards and nothing more:\n\n```\nham.001 -> 10.10.11.1014.01.ham\nham.002 -> 10.10.11.1026.01.ham\nham.003 -> 10.10.11.1145.01.ham\n```\n\nBrand numbers are never reused or renumbered.\n\n## Brand Cards\n\nA brand card is titled with the word and lives on the face 01 of the page that explains it. It holds:\n- the surface forms it covers (case-sensitive): `Ham, Hammie, hammie`\n- what it means: \"user's friend from work\"\n- **meters:** the top 5 words found with it, and how often each appears when this meaning is used. For `ham.001`: sandwich 100%, lunch 95%, hungry 75%, eat 50%, meal 43%.\n\nTo resolve a word, the compute finds it in the dictionary, reads each brand card, and compares the sentence against the meters.\n\n## Local First\n\nEach book keeps its own dictionary. The kernel supplies a small base dictionary that books inherit. Linking dictionaries to other books (source books) is a later design issue. Brand numbers `000` and `999` are reserved and unused; brands run 001-998.\n\n## Meters\n\nThe compute records each resolution of a word: the brand chosen, the sentence's words, and the outcome. Meters are calculated from these records. They are shown on the card, readable by a person, never written by hand, and exempt from drafting (D0).\n\n| Outcome | Signal |\n|---|---|\n| success | the output runs and the user carries on |\n| failure | negative words; the user stops the process; **restatement**: the user repeats and over-explains because the first attempt failed |\n\nOn a failure, the compute updates the brand cards linked to that resolution.\n\nOpen: does a failure count only against a brand that was assumed (recent or default) rather than matched by meters? Proposed: yes, and a repeated failure triggers the probe in C8.8.\n\n## Resolving A Word\n\n1. The sentence matches one brand's meters clearly: use it.\n2. No clear match: use the brand last used in this session.\n3. None used yet: use the default marked in the dictionary entry.\n4. No default, or no brand at all: use the LLM's ordinary meaning.\n\nAny assumption is stated back (\"ham, taken as ham.001, the sandwich\").\n\nWhen the user shows a meaning the dictionary does not have, the compute pushes the model to ask for it. The model hands the answer to the compute, which creates the brand card and the dictionary entry. The new card goes through drafting (D0); the user confirming the meaning is its review.\n\n## Keywords Are Brands\n\nThe model proposes keywords for a card and hands them to the compute, which stores each one in the dictionary with what it states. If a matching meaning already exists, it is reused. See C8.","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0055"}
{"n":24,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0055.02.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"R9 struck rows from it in this pass; brand-card 04 rule is open (Q11). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Dictionary Layout\nsource: docs/spec/decisions.md:446-481\n\nOne layout, the same from the kernel out. Sections are never re-split. Each letter gets 1-5 sections by its share of English words (PlainSpell, Wiktionary, 2026): S at 10.7% gets 5, so each section holds about 2.1%.\n\n| Range | Use |\n|---|---|\n| `03.01-03.09` | reserved: words starting with digits or symbols |\n| `03.10-03.61` | letters, below |\n| `03.62-03.98` | reserved |\n\n| Letter | Sections |\n|---|---|\n| A | `10` a-ak, `11` al-ap, `12` aq-az |\n| B | `13` ba-bh, `14` bi-bq, `15` br-bz |\n| C | `16` ca-cg, `17` ch-cn, `18` co-con, `19` coo-cz |\n| D | `20` da-de, `21` df-di, `22` dj-dz |\n| E | `23` ea-em, `24` en-ez |\n| F | `25` fa-fk, `26` fl-fz |\n| G | `27` ga-gk, `28` gl-gz |\n| H | `29` ha-hh, `30` hi-hz |\n| I | `31` ia-im, `32` in-iz |\n| J, K | `33`, `34` |\n| L | `35` la-lh, `36` li-lz |\n| M | `37` ma, `38` mb-mi, `39` mj-mz |\n| N, O | `40`, `41` |\n| P | `42` pa-pd, `43` pe-pk, `44` pl-pq, `45` pr-pz |\n| Q | `46` |\n| R | `47` ra-re, `48` rf-rz |\n| S | `49` sa-sc, `50` sd-sh, `51` si-so, `52` sp-st, `53` su-sz |\n| T | `54` ta-th, `55` ti-tz |\n| U, V, W, X, Y, Z | `56`, `57`, `58`, `59`, `60`, `61` |\n\n- A word is placed by its lowercase form; case still separates brands inside the entry.\n- Missing sections are valid (A4.6): a small book creates only what it needs.\n- Within-letter boundaries are estimates and are checked against a real word list before the layout is frozen.\n- This layout reserves `03.01-03.09` by its own definition. A1.4 (the reserved range does not apply to climbing counters) still stands.\n\n## Index Card\nsource: docs/spec/brands.md:23-30\n\n```\n## ham\ntype: index\ndefault: ham.003\nham.001: 10.10.11.1014.01.ham\nham.002: 10.10.11.1026.01.ham\nham.003: 10.10.11.1145.01.ham\n```\n\n## Brand Card Form\nsource: docs/spec/brands.md:34-41\n\n```\n## ham\ntype: brand\nbrand: ham.001\nforms: ham\nmeans: user's favourite food, usually a sandwich\nmeters: sandwich 100%, lunch 95%, hungry 75%, eat 50%, meal 43%\n```","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0055"}
{"n":25,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0055.04.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Brands\nsource: [01 / Brands] decisions.md:418; 04 text: brands.md:7; why: docs/spec/brands.md:7\nwhy: Relying on a model's average meaning is not good enough.\n\nA word can mean different things to different people. Relying on a model's average meaning is not good enough. \"Ham\" might be lunch meat, a friend's nickname, or Happy Arctic Men. When a user explains their meaning of a word, it is recorded, so models look it up instead of guessing.\n\n## Card Key Address\nsource: [01 / Card Key Address] decisions.md:421; 04 text: brands.md:47\nwhy: 4.7\n\nA word after a face is a card key: `10.10.11.1014.01.ham`. A 3-digit group after it is a brand: `...0012.03.ham.002`. Tail words are reserved, so card keys never collide with them. [C8.2]\n\n## Dictionary Is An Index\nsource: [01 / Dictionary Is An Index] decisions.md:424-432; 04 text: brands.md:11-15\nwhy: 4.7\n\n| Part | Where | Holds |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Brand** | `word.NNN` | one meaning of a word; `001-998` (`000` and `999` reserved) |\n| **Brand card** | face 01 of the page that explains the meaning | forms, meaning, meters |\n| **Dictionary** | page `0012`, face 03 | an index from each word to its brand cards, nothing more |\n\n## Brand Cards\nsource: [01 / Brand Cards] decisions.md:435-440; 04 text: brands.md:32-43\nwhy: 4.7\n\nBrand card on `10.10.11.1014.01`:\n\n```\n## ham\ntype: brand\nbrand: ham.001\nforms: ham\nmeans: user's favourite food, usually a sandwich\nmeters: sandwich 100%, lunch 95%, hungry 75%, eat 50%, meal 43%\n```\n\nThe other two cards: `ham.002` (forms `Ham, Hammie, hammie`; a friend from work) and `ham.003` (form `HAM`; a Facebook group, Happy Arctic Men).\n\n## Local First\nsource: [01 / Local First] decisions.md:443; 04 text: brands.md:78\nwhy: 4.7\n\nEach book keeps its own dictionary. The kernel provides a small base dictionary that books inherit. Referencing other books' dictionaries (source books) is a later design.\n\n## Meters\nsource: [01 / Meters] decisions.md:484-493; 04 text: brands.md:55-61\nwhy: 4.7\n\nThe top 5 words that appear with this meaning, and how often each appears when this meaning is used. Only the compute writes meters (C8.7):\n- Every resolution is recorded: word, brand chosen, sentence words, outcome.\n- **Success:** the output runs and the user carries on.\n- **Failure:** negative words, the user stopping the process, or **restatement** (the user repeating and over-explaining because the first attempt failed).\n- On a failure, the compute updates the brand cards linked to that resolution.\n\nMeters are readable on the card but never edited by hand, and are exempt from drafting.\n\n## Resolving A Word\nsource: [01 / Resolving A Word] decisions.md:496-503; 04 text: brands.md:65-70\nwhy: 4.7\n\n1. The sentence matches one brand's meters clearly: use it.\n2. No clear match: the brand last used in this session.\n3. None used yet: the dictionary entry's `default`.\n4. No default, or no brand: the LLM's ordinary meaning.\n\nEvery assumption is stated back (\"ham, taken as ham.001, the sandwich\").\n\n## Keywords Are Brands\nsource: [01 / Keywords Are Brands] decisions.md:360\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## Dictionary Layout\nsource: [02 / Dictionary Layout] decisions.md:446-481; 04 text: brands.md:82-92\nwhy: 4.7\n\nOne fixed layout from the kernel out, never re-split. Each letter has 1-5 sections by its share of English words.\n\n| Range | Use |\n|---|---|\n| `03.01-03.09` | reserved: digits and symbols |\n| `03.10-03.61` | letters (A `10-12`, B `13-15`, C `16-19`, D `20-22`, E `23-24`, F `25-26`, G `27-28`, H `29-30`, I `31-32`, J `33`, K `34`, L `35-36`, M `37-39`, N `40`, O `41`, P `42-45`, Q `46`, R `47-48`, S `49-53`, T `54-55`, U-Z `56-61`) |\n| `03.62-03.98` | reserved |\n\nThe full split within each letter is in `decisions.md` C8.6. The within-letter boundaries are estimates, to be checked against a real word list before freezing.\n\n## Index Card\nsource: [02 / Index Card] brands.md:23-30\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## Brand Card Form\nsource: [02 / Brand Card Form] brands.md:34-41\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0055"}
{"n":26,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0055.06.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Seed Session\n\nPrima seed session, 2026-11-11. Note fields are open (decisions.md C7), so this note carries none (`7`).\n\n- R9: `03.00` (menu) and `03.99` rows struck from the dictionary layout. `7`: the dictionary menu has no written home; its entries are derived by the compute (decisions.md:516). If it needs one, it would be a card on face 03 (not done).\n- Open, copied: Open: does a failure count only against a brand that was assumed (recent or default) rather than matched by meters? Proposed: yes, and a repeated failure triggers the probe in C8.8.\n- Open, copied: A brand card lives on face 01 of a page that explains the meaning. When the page does not exist yet: the model asks the user which page, or each book has a default terms page.\n- `7`: the kernel base dictionary (decisions.md C8.5 \"The kernel supplies a small base dictionary that books inherit\") has no listed words, so page 0012 is not seated.\n- Merges from Director rulings (docs/spec/DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md: R1 door, R9 no face sentinels, R10 face 00 registry), one per change, copied text otherwise unchanged:\n  - R9 [01 / Dictionary Is An Index]: \"`0012.03.00` is the menu of sections.\" -> \"\"\n  - R9 [02 / Dictionary Layout]: \"| `03.00` | menu |\" -> \"\"\n  - R9 [02 / Dictionary Layout]: \"| `03.99` | continuation |\" -> \"\"\n  - R9 [04 / Dictionary Layout]: \"| `03.00` | menu |\" -> \"\"\n  - R9 [04 / Dictionary Layout]: \"| `03.99` | continuation |\" -> \"\"","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0055"}
{"n":27,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0056.00.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"---\naddress: 00.00.00.0056\ntitle: drafting\nstate: draft\ntype: system\n---\n\n## Registry\nformat: 7\n\nR10 (DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md:40): face 00 is the registry, the place that lists the page's faces. Layout is `7` (not given).\n\n- `00.draft` registry: draft because registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `01.draft` law: draft because 2 of its 3 cards have a missing why on 04. Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `02.draft` schema: draft because the drafting mechanism is proposed (decisions.md:320). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `04.draft` user: draft because holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `06.01.draft` notes: draft because this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0056"}
{"n":28,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0056.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"2 of its 3 cards have a missing why on 04. Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Never From Memory\n\nA model must not rewrite a face from memory. Every change goes through a draft that starts as a copy and is changed one note at a time:\n\n1. Copy the existing face to `FF.draft`.\n2. Add notes from memory to the draft. Notes, not edits.\n3. Check for additional sources and add notes from them.\n4. Merge the notes one at a time, striking each as it is merged.\n5. Record overflow and unprompted ideas as new notes. Once all notes are merged, go on.\n6. Read the whole face again and write notes again. A full view is needed to write well.\n7. Merge and strike those notes.\n8. Read again. If there are no more notes, hand back for review.\n\n## New Face\n\nA brand-new face has nothing to copy. It starts as an empty draft and still goes through notes and merges.\n\n## Exceptions\n\n- **Meters** on brand cards are written only by the compute and are not drafted (`brands.md`).\n- **Derived content** (contents lists, menus, ancestor paths) is calculated and never drafted.\n- **A new brand card** is drafted by the compute from the user's stated meaning; the user confirming the meaning is its review.","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0056"}
{"n":29,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0056.02.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"the drafting mechanism is proposed (decisions.md:320). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Drafting Ops\nsource: docs/spec/decisions.md:320-330\n\nProposed: the compute enforces this through its operations, so a face cannot be replaced from memory:\n\n| Op | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `copy` | the compute copies the current seated face to `FF.draft`. The writer supplies no body. |\n| `note` | add one note to a draft |\n| `merge` | apply one change to the draft body, citing the note it comes from; that note is struck |\n| `promote` | after review, the draft replaces the seated face |\n\n- A full-body `put` is allowed only to create a new face, never to replace an existing one.\n- Every change to a draft traces back to a note, and review can check that every note is struck.","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0056"}
{"n":30,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0056.04.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Never From Memory\nsource: [01 / Never From Memory] decisions.md:309-318; 04 text: drafting.md:7; why: docs/spec/drafting.md:7\nwhy: A rewrite from memory silently drops what the model did not remember.\n\nA model must never rewrite a face from memory. A rewrite from memory silently drops what the model did not remember. Every change starts from a copy of the real text and arrives one note at a time, so nothing is lost and every change can be traced.\n\n## New Face\nsource: [01 / New Face] drafting.md:20\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## Exceptions\nsource: [01 / Exceptions] drafting.md:39-41\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## Drafting Ops\nsource: [02 / Drafting Ops] decisions.md:320-330; 04 text: drafting.md:24-35; why: docs/spec/drafting.md:7\nwhy: Every change starts from a copy of the real text and arrives one note at a time, so nothing is lost and every change can be traced.\n\nThe compute only accepts changes to an existing face through these operations, so a face cannot be replaced from memory:\n\n| Op | Step | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| `copy` | 1 | the compute copies the seated face to `FF.draft`; the writer sends no body |\n| `note` | 2, 3, 5, 6 | add one note to the draft |\n| `merge` | 4, 7 | apply one change to the draft, citing its note; the note is struck |\n| `promote` | after review | the draft replaces the seated face |\n\n- `put` (a full body) is allowed only to create a new face, never to replace one.\n- Every change traces to a note. Review checks that every note is struck before `promote`.\n- The log keeps every note and merge, so the full history of a face can be read back.","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0056"}
{"n":31,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0056.06.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Seed Session\n\nPrima seed session, 2026-11-11. Note fields are open (decisions.md C7), so this note carries none (`7`).\n\n- Open, copied (drafting.md):\n\n- How review is recorded, and what happens when review rejects a draft (section D).\n- Whether a page type may turn drafting off, for example for scratch pages (C1.4 allows it as a type rule).\n- Merges from Director rulings (docs/spec/DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md: R1 door, R9 no face sentinels, R10 face 00 registry), one per change, copied text otherwise unchanged:\n  - R9 [01 / Exceptions]: \"(boards, contents lists\" -> \"(contents lists\"","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0056"}
{"n":32,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0057.00.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"---\naddress: 00.00.00.0057\ntitle: workflow-loop\nstate: draft\ntype: system\n---\n\n## Registry\nformat: 7\n\nR10 (DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md:40): face 00 is the registry, the place that lists the page's faces. Layout is `7` (not given).\n\n- `00.draft` registry: draft because registry and stamp; the stamp's `state` and `type` values are open (QUESTIONS Q8; MAP hole 4). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `01.draft` law: draft because copies workflow-loop.003.md, which says \"state: draft - Director review required; not live\" (:3). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `02.draft` schema: draft because copies workflow-loop.003.md, which says \"state: draft - Director review required; not live\" (:3). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `04.draft` user: draft because holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).\n- `06.01.draft` notes: draft because this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0057"}
{"n":33,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0057.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"copies workflow-loop.003.md, which says \"state: draft - Director review required; not live\" (:3). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Roles\n\n| Role | Who | Does | Does not |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Person | the Director, or whoever holds the level | approves a specific N4 revision, its frame and delegation; authorizes governing changes; answers elevations | delegation cannot exceed their own authority |\n| Lead | a model, once the N4 page is approved; or a person | reads N4, writes packs, reads returns, runs passes, drafts changes to 01/04 | change 01 or 04 without approval |\n| Builder | a model, a person, or compute | claims a pack, does its work cards, writes the return | write outside the pack's grant |\n| Reviewer | a view other than the builder **(approved S10)** | independently checks evidence against acceptance criteria; challenges assumptions; uses N7 for required higher-risk trials | review its own work; accept the builder's summary as proof |\n| Compute | the compute | resolves approved revisions, enforces grants on its own writes, verifies external changes, creates handoffs, lints records, moves tails, counts passes, records claims | decide a disputed question or infer an approval |\n\nA lead is not authority (axiom 3). The lead works inside what the approved N4 page grants (axiom 1).\n\n## Band Design\n\nThe workflow map defaults within the standard range `0100-0999` (A2.7, SeqAddr section 6.1). `N4` means the dev role. In the default map it is `0400-0499`. A user band in `1000-9998` adopts a workflow role by name in its band definition. `1000` is the first user band head.\n\n| Band | Name | Holds |\n|---|---|---|\n| `0000-0099` | existing kernel allocation | separate from the workflow map |\n| `0100-0299` | unassigned by this workflow | follows governing band definitions |\n| `0300-0399` (N3) | build | modules built, with explanation. Face 01: what the module is. Face 02: the module's files described (section 3.1). Face 08: links to the files in the repo |\n| `0400-0499` (N4) | dev | the design: what the work is, its MVP, goals, what we are trying to prove. Person and model talk here. Face 04 mostly, with 01 to go with it |\n| `0500-0599` (N5) | jobs | packs and returns, as face 05 counters |\n| `0600-0699` | unassigned by this workflow | follows governing band definitions |\n| `0700-0799` (N7) | colab | labs: cold-model trials of the page's work |\n| `0800-0999` | unassigned by this workflow | follows governing band definitions |\n\n`N?00` denotes a hundred-page band head, such as `0400` or the user band `1500`. Ordinary subject work starts at `N?01`. The notation explains the existing SeqAddr grammar.\n\n**S05, F02-F03.** The proposed [F.2 amendment](decisions.md#f2-a26-amendment-for-workflow-sets) records the map and the change to A2.6. `7`: reconcile the affected A2.6 and A6.1 wording and inherited schemas through their own drafting path before enabling the amended rules.\n\n## Bound Bands\n\n- A matching two-digit subject number helps find counterparts. Example: design `0401.timer`, packs and returns `0501.timer`, build `0301.timer`, labs `0701.timer`.\n- Under proposed F.2, membership requires reciprocal Face 08 links (axiom 8). This amends A2.6's current membership by number.\n- **(filled)** The compute checks the set: a set page that is named but cannot be opened is `7.8`, raised, never created from a lookalike.\n- **(filled)** Lineage runs through the set: from a build back to its pack, from the pack back to the design, from the design back to its approval (axiom 6).\n- **S05.** Overflow follows SeqAddr section 7.1. The `PP99` pointer names the next allocated hundred for the same role and has explicit Face 08 forward and back-links. Follow the pointer's recorded target.\n- If a continuation changes subject numbering, record its predecessor and set membership explicitly. The original identities remain immutable. `7`: the `continues-from` link kind remains proposed under C4/E9.\n\n## Code In The Repo\n\nDecided by the Director (2026-11-11): code lives in the repo, not in the book.\n\n- The N3 page describes the module: face 01 what it is, face 02 its files, what each does and its interface.\n- Face 08 links each file by its repo path. A work card's `path` is the same repo path.\n- **(filled)** Each link records the commit it was last checked at, so the build page's lineage can be followed in the repo's history (axiom 6).\n- **(approved S02)** The return cites exact changed paths and Git commit or working-tree revision plus verification evidence. A claimed change without inspectable evidence is not accepted merely because the builder reported `done`.\n- **(filled)** The compute checks that each linked path exists. A path that cannot be opened is `7.8`, raised, never repointed to a similar file.\n- **(filled)** The book keeps the why; the repo keeps the code. If the code rots, the why on N4 and N3 is enough to rebuild it.\n\n**F08.** For Compute-owned writes, Compute checks authority and scope before executing, then verifies and records the result. For external Git or filesystem writes, Compute validates declared targets before work and verifies actual changes against the pack's recorded baseline afterward. Compare additions, modifications, removals, renames and untracked files within the verification workspace. Record pre-existing changes with the baseline. Use both sides of a rename when checking the grant.\n\nIf a change falls outside the grant or its attribution is uncertain, preserve the evidence, report the finding and block acceptance and promotion pending authorized remediation. The return can still record what happened. `7`: define a reliable workspace boundary and attribution policy. A shared diff shows differences, and concurrent writers can make their origin ambiguous. An isolated worktree or exclusive workspace is a candidate for the first implementation.\n\n## N4 Approval\n\n- N4 states the work: the MVP for this page, the goals, what we are trying to prove. Written on face 04, with an agents.md-style face 01 to go with it.\n- **(approved S01)** The person approves **one identified N4 revision** with its acceptance criteria, permitted scope, approving authority, and delegation terms. Approval is a lineage record with a stable identifier, not a mutable flag on the page.\n- **(approved S04)** Within the explicitly approved N4 scope, the lead may direct passes and authorize routine completion and promotion **only to the extent delegated**. Promotion still requires independent trial and compute checks. Governing changes, changed acceptance criteria, expanded grants, or material changes outside the approved frame require elevation and fresh approval.\n- A pack links to the exact approval record and N4 base revision. Compute refuses an unrelated, stale, or insufficient grant; it does not guess whether a later N4 edit was approved.\n- The recorded grant supplies the workflow's explicit operation scope. Altering law, changing Face 01/04, or expanding the frame follows the applicable authority and drafting rules. Check `1.7.8` before each write. `7`: the general E8 definition of written permission remains open.\n- **(approved S12)** N4 Face 05 holds design questions; N5 Face 05 holds execution packs. These are independent counted faces, not mirrored counters. Every related question and pack uses explicit Face 08 links to its counterpart, where one exists.\n- N4 Face 06 holds the lead's session/pass notes. A note references the exact reviewed N5 return and N4 revision it was written against. Matching counter numbers may help a reader but are **never evidence of connection**.\n\n## Packs\n\nA pack is a face 05 counter on the N5 page: `0501.05.NN`. It is an agents.md-style build list for a builder. `05.01` is the first place a pack can be written.\n\n## Risk And Governing Targets\n\n**F06-F07.** Compute derives a floor from the registered target type, operation, grant and authority metadata. The effective risk is the higher of that floor and the lead's proposal. Record the inputs and reason so a reviewer can reproduce the decision.\n\nA governing change edits a registered governing target or declares a change to governing behavior. Targets include the Axioms, SeqAddr grammar, kernel contracts, type definitions, authority, inheritance and workflow lifecycle rules. A source-code change can affect the same behavior. An unknown target or disputed behavioral impact is raised for classification before routine delegation can be used. Governing changes require Director or applicable governing authority approval and a recorded N7 trial before promotion.\n\n`7`: the target registry, risk rubric, routine eligibility and N7 thresholds beyond governing changes. Until those are defined, the lead's rating alone cannot establish review or promotion eligibility.\n\n## The Loop\n\n```\ndraft -> pack -> open -> return -> review -> closed\n```\n\n`closed` is the label for the **bare** `05.NN` counter. Its separately recorded `outcome` may be `passed`, `failed`, `blocked`, `needs-authority`, or `abandoned` **(approved S03)**.\n\n| Step | Tail | Who | What happens | axioms |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 Write | `draft` | lead | writes the pack and its work cards | 5 |\n| 2 Lint | `draft -> pack` | compute | checks the pack against its template (section 8). A pack that fails stays `draft` | 3C |\n| 3 Claim | `pack -> open` | builder via compute | atomic first valid claim wins. Record owner, claim ID, start and recovery terms. Record the E4 raise before work if the builder differs from the lead. | 1.3.6 |\n| 4 Work | `open` | builder | completes each work card. Asks questions; applies for permission or solutions to what blocks it; stops at `stop if` | 5.1 |\n| 5 Return | `open -> return` | builder via Compute, or Compute during authorized recovery | records the return with per-card results or verified recovery facts and the evidence available | 5.6 |\n| 6 Review | `return -> review` | Compute on a valid reviewer claim | records reviewer identity, the exact return revision and review scope. While the card is in `review`, the reviewer checks the artifacts and evidence and records the finding and outcome. | 9 |\n| 7 Close | `review -> bare counter` | Compute | moves the card to Closed once the review and its outcome are recorded. The card retains the history of every stage. | 6.7 |\n| 8 Lead pass | | lead | reads, notes, elevates, decides the next pack (section 7) | 6.4 |\n\n## Return Placement\n\nThe `.003` workflow draft uses the E9 proposal to append the return to the same counter as the pack. The earlier pack body remains in the log. `7`: seat the E9 placement and specify how the return body and `open -> return` become visible as one durable change or recover to a consistent state after interruption.\n\nCompute records a structurally valid return from a current claim even when the record shows missing proof, a failure, a blocker or a grant violation. Review uses those findings to decide the outcome. A malformed or stale submission cannot advance the tail. Preserve the reason for refusal and use the authorized recovery path if the claim can no longer produce a valid return.\n\n## Review (filled)\n\n- **S10, F09.** Record reviewer identity and context, what they saw first, the checks and hostile attempts they performed, and the evidence and finding for each. The builder is ineligible to review its own return. Compare actual artifacts and changes against the approved criteria and grant. For a replacement, open the predecessor read-only (E7).\n- Proposed review classes: R1 uses a separate reviewer for routine work. R2 uses fresh context and opens approved criteria and the artifact before the builder's conclusions. R3 adds a recorded hostile N7 trial for governing work, with a reviewer separate from both builder and pack author. Governing changes require N7 before promotion. `7`: settle the identity evidence, when a same-model session counts under E7, and risk thresholds beyond governing changes. Every promotion still requires the E7 trial.\n- **S03.** `passed` requires satisfied pack criteria and resolved violations. `blocked` records a missing prerequisite. `needs-authority` records a next step outside the grant. `failed` records unmet criteria or rejected evidence or scope. `abandoned` requires the verified recovery record in section 6.4. Record the outcome and why on the transition to the bare counter.\n- A failed review closes the counter. Rework gets a new counter and an explicit predecessor reference. `7`: the formal `continues-from` link kind remains proposed (C4/E9).\n\n## Open While Gone\n\n**S11, F04.** A claim records its ID, owner, start, last checkpoint or heartbeat if available, and recovery policy. Silence alone leaves the claim unresolved. After the required authority records an abandonment decision, Compute fences the old claim and records a system return on the same counter. Include the reason, last verified checkpoint, observed changes, unverified changes and outstanding work. Attribute only facts supported by evidence. The system return proceeds through `review` before closing with outcome `abandoned`. Replacement work uses a new counter, an explicit predecessor reference and a fresh authorization check.\n\n`7`: define who may confirm abandonment, timeout and offline policies, and the fencing mechanism. Fencing can reject the old claim in Compute. For external writes, separately protect the replacement workspace from late changes by the original builder.\n\n## Lead Pass\n\nEach pass, before writing a new pack, the lead:\n\n1. **Reads N4 01 and 04,** the notes on N4 face 06, and the reviewed return. Every pass. (Director) The read is pinned to the active approved N4 revision, not whatever content happens to be newest **(approved S01)**.\n2. **Writes a note** to N4 Face 06: what the return taught, what changed, why, and an explicit link to its N5 return. Note counters need not mirror job counters **(approved S12)**.\n3. **Elevates changes to 01 and 04.** When they need updating, the lead does not change them. It elevates with suggested changes, as a draft, and the person approves (axioms 1 and 9; drafting).\n4. **Separates three outcomes (approved S04):** (a) individual pack passed review, (b) the accumulated MVP satisfies approved acceptance criteria, and (c) an authorized promotion may occur. The lead recommends completion; compute checks objective evidence and delegation; the person decides any matter reserved by the approval. If unfinished and permitted, the lead writes a focused next pack.\n5. **(approved S06)** Before each cold builder handoff, compute derives a read-only manifest from the live records: approved N4 revision and grant, pack counter, predecessor return(s), current source commits/revisions, unresolved blockers, required faces/cards, acceptance criteria, and stop conditions. A manifest is a **view**, not a new authority; it identifies exact source links and is regenerated when stale.\n\n## Promotion Manifest\n\n**F10.** A promotion manifest names the exact artifact revisions eligible for publication. It records the pack and reviewed return revision, N4 revision, frame and approval, exact source revisions and SeqAddr records, the review evidence and required trial, exclusions and unresolved findings, and the promotion authorization. It also identifies the expected live predecessors. A handoff manifest supplies builder inputs under S06.\n\nBefore publishing, Compute checks that the manifest scope is covered by the recorded authorization and that the artifact and predecessor revisions still match. A changed artifact must be reviewed at its new revision. `7`: specify how validation and publication share a revision guard, and how a mixed Git and book promotion recovers from partial publication. Hold mixed-store promotion until that contract is defined.\n\n## Gravity\n\nThe number of passes is an aim, not a quota. 1-3 passes is normal. Models left alone loop on one detail, so there are hard move-on points.\n\n**S09, F05.** Compute counts passes under the approved N4 frame and independently tracks repeated attempts against an unresolved blocker. Changing lead, counter or blocker label does not reset the frame count. Proposed counting event: each successful `draft -> pack` release consumes one pass exactly once. `7`: specify how release and accounting commit together and how blocker identity survives revision and relabeling.\n\n| Next pass in the frame's current allowance | What happens before release |\n|---|---|\n| 1-3 | normal |\n| 4 | Compute requires the why questions. The lead answers and Compute records the answers as an N4 06 note. |\n| 5 | Keep the pass-4 requirement. The lead records new evidence or a changed method against the earlier answers. |\n| 6 | Halt before release. Raise the named frame, blocker history, and recorded answers to the authority. |\n\nA decision responding to the recorded halt can continue the frame for a stated allowance, revise the frame with fresh approval, delegate within a stated scope and allowance, or stop. Proposed default: a reply such as \"carry on\" tied to the halt and given by the authority for that frame grants one additional release when it specifies no count. Continued releases carry the pass-5 check. Halt again when the allowance is exhausted. Unrelated conversation leaves the halt in force. Retain the previous counts and reasons in lineage. `7`: confirm the default allowance and restart policy.\n\n**(approved S09)** Compute records a possible loop when two passes repeat the same target, request, and unchanged evidence. Matching `path` and `ask` alone is a finding. The next pass must cite new evidence, a changed hypothesis or method, or explicit authorization. The pass-5 check and any continuation checks still apply.","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0057"}
{"n":34,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0057.02.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"copies workflow-loop.003.md, which says \"state: draft - Director review required; not live\" (:3). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Pack Header\nsource: docs/spec/workflow-loop.003.md:103-116\n\n| Field | Holds | axioms |\n|---|---|---|\n| `by` | who made the pack | 6 |\n| `authorized` | immutable approval ID and approved N4 revision **(approved S01)** | 1.6 |\n| `lead` | explicit lead letters unless a recorded band default applies. `7`: no `3MC` workflow default is seated. | 3 |\n| `axioms` | the chain for the whole pack **(filled)** | |\n| `outcome` | what done looks like | 5 |\n| `overview` | what the work is trying to achieve; matches N4 01 and 04 | 5.2 |\n| `stop if` | where the builder stops and raises **(filled)** | 5.1 |\n| `grant` | explicit allowed operations and target addresses/path patterns, bounded by the approval **(approved S01, S08)** | 1 |\n| `frame` | approved MVP/work-frame ID for gravity accounting **(approved S09)** | 5.6 |\n| `risk` | lead's proposed risk. Compute records its floor and the effective risk (section 5.3). | 5.9 |\n| `predecessor` | previous pack or issue and expected artifact revisions, if any **(approved S06)** | 6.8 |\n| repository baseline | starting source revision and pre-existing workspace changes for external work (section 3.1). `7`: exact record format. | 5.6 |\n\n## Work Card\nsource: docs/spec/workflow-loop.003.md:120-146\n\nThe original calls each piece of work `#NN`. A model can add as many as the work needs, which is good: the pack grows with the work.\n\n**(approved S07)** The work-card key is lowercase `w01`, `w02`, ... (the title-form key required by SeqAddr). `W01` in older examples is a historical label, not the new canonical key.\n- `NN` already means a counter (`05.NN`), so `#NN` inside a counter reads as two counters.\n- `#NN` is also used for jobs released in talk (`#03 >run`, `claude/readpath.md`).\n- `CC` clashes with C for compute in the lead letters (`3CM`).\n- `w` distinguishes work cards from face counters. Use a heading `## w01` so the normalized key is precisely `w01`; place the description in a `title:` field, not in the heading. A work card is a SeqAddr card with `type: work`.\n\nEach work card:\n\n```text\n## w01\ntype: work\ntitle: build the lead command\naxioms: 5.4\nreason: A person needs one place to inspect what the builder completed and what still blocks the job.\nrecommend: In jobs.py, add a command that takes Did, Blocked, and Need and writes them as three lines before the first work card on a return. It refuses a counter that is not a return. Stamp the change. Keep the older copy.\npath: 10.workshop/10.10.work/10.10.10.lib/code/jobs.py\nrisk: 2\nlead: 3C\nask: >build Add the lead command.\n```\n\n- `reason` states why the work is needed (axiom 4). A work card with no reason fails lint. When the reason depends on prior work, cite the exact predecessor revision or full SeqAddr reference. The example is illustrative.\n- **(approved S02, S10)** Evidence is proportional to likely harm, reversibility and scope. A low-risk documentation correction may need a diff and lint. A governing change requires a recorded independent N7 trial before promotion. The initial 1-5 scale and a level 4-5 cutoff remain candidates under section 5.3.\n- `lead` only when it differs from the pack's.\n- `axioms` optional, and encouraged: it is the quick why for the builder.\n\n## Return Shape\nsource: docs/spec/workflow-loop.003.md:189-193\n\n- Per work card: `w01` with `result: done | blocked | need`; list exact changed file paths or full SeqAddr addresses and **inspectable evidence** (test command/output, diff, commit, or other relevant proof). Record deviations and their why **(approved S02)**.\n- Evidence is proportional to risk; compute validates presence and references, while an independent reviewer assesses whether it actually proves the claim. Tests failing must be disclosed, not rebranded as success.\n- A `blocked` card supplies the missing prerequisite. A `need` card identifies the operation and authority needed. Use these to support `blocked` and `needs-authority` outcomes, respectively. If blockers and missing authority coexist, record all of them and the why for the outcome selected. `7`: decide whether mixed outcomes require a deterministic precedence rule.\n- Then: questions, permission asked, solutions proposed to what blocked it.\n- Then, at the end, each with its why: **notes**, **unprompted** (one thing the builder would have done differently), **overflow** (what was seen outside the frame, reported and not acted on).\n\n## Pack Lint\nsource: docs/spec/workflow-loop.003.md:232-252\n\nCompute uses templates for packs, work cards and returns. This draft proposes kernel-owned card types under C8.9. Any permitted specialization needs a named override under axiom 2. `7`: seat the workflow card types and templates through the kernel's drafting path. Consult the predecessor `0117.pack-lint`.\n\n**(filled)** What the lint checks:\n\n| Check | On | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| header has `by`, `authorized`, `outcome`, `overview`, `stop if`, `grant`, `frame`, `risk` | pack | hard: stays `draft` |\n| `authorized` resolves to an approved, specific N4 revision and the grant covers the requested work. Verify promotion delegation when promotion is requested. | pack, promotion | hard |\n| every `wNN` card has `type: work`, `title`, `reason`, declared target intent, and `ask` **(S07, S08)** | pack | hard |\n| intended targets and operations are inside the grant; Compute-owned writes are checked before execution; actual external changes are checked against the baseline **(S08, F08)** | pack, write, review | hard before controlled writes and before acceptance. Violations remain recordable. |\n| **existing dependency** must open; **modify target** must exist and be writable; **create target** must be legal and available, not already present **(S08)** | pack | hard, with `7.8` for required missing dependencies |\n| `axioms:` chains parse | pack, card | hard |\n| a builder return records each work-card result and its touched targets or explicitly identifies what is unknown. Review checks `done` against proportional evidence. Recovery returns follow section 6.4. | return, review | hard for malformed return shape. Missing proof or out-of-grant changes block acceptance. |\n| notes, unprompted and overflow present (may be empty, with a why) | return | finding |\n| reviewer identity is recorded and distinct from builder | review | hard before claiming review |\n| the E7 trial, mandatory N7 for governing changes, and artifact-specific authorization resolve | promotion | hard before publication |\n| claim and expected revision remain valid; stale claims cannot write **(S01, S11)** | transitions | hard |\n| material N4 design updates do not reuse old approval without a renewed grant **(S01)** | pack, promotion | hard |\n| a work card with no `axioms:` line | pack | finding |\n\nRecord the reason for a refused transition or acceptance. Preserve findings in the return and review so a refusal remains inspectable.\n\n## Why Questions\nsource: docs/spec/workflow-loop.003.md:273-278\n\n1. Why does this need another pass? (4)\n2. What did the last return change, in one sentence? (6)\n3. Is the frame the right size, and does it let the work finish? (5)\n4. Is the remaining gap worth its cost, in proportion to being wrong? (4)\n5. Is this the same detail as the last pass? If so, why will this pass differ? (9)\n6. What is missing that only the person can give? (7.1)","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0057"}
{"n":35,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0057.04.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"holds missing whys (`why: 4.7`) or `7` hole bodies; a required absence (decisions.md C3.2). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Roles\nsource: [01 / Roles] workflow-loop.003.md:24-32\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## Band Design\nsource: [01 / Band Design] workflow-loop.003.md:38-53; 04 text: decisions.md:644-645; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:631\nwhy: Matching subject digits can help a reader find related pages. Continuation must follow the actual pointer and backlinks.\n\n- In a workflow that adopts the map in `.003` section 2, matching `N` and `NN` identify candidates for a set. Membership requires explicit reciprocal Face 08 links. Matching titles help a person inspect the set.\n- The workflow map defaults within `0100-0999`: `0300` build, `0400` dev, `0500` jobs, and `0700` colab. A user band adopts a workflow role by name in its band definition. A2.7's allocation stays in force.\n\n## Bound Bands\nsource: [01 / Bound Bands] workflow-loop.003.md:59-64; why: docs/spec/decisions.md:648\nwhy: Axiom 8 requires written connections for lawful traversal.\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## Code In The Repo\nsource: [01 / Code In The Repo] workflow-loop.003.md:70-81; why: docs/spec/workflow-loop.003.md:77\nwhy: The book keeps the why; the repo keeps the code. If the code rots, the why on N4 and N3 is enough to rebuild it.\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## N4 Approval\nsource: [01 / N4 Approval] workflow-loop.003.md:87-93; 04 text: decisions.md:625-627 (F.1 S01); why: docs/spec/decisions.md:627\nwhy: A later N4 edit must not inherit authority by reusing the same page address.\n\n| Decision | Approved direction and scope | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| S01 | Bind every workflow grant to an approval ID, exact N4 revision, frame, criteria, scope, and delegation. | A later N4 edit must not inherit authority by reusing the same page address. |\n\n## Packs\nsource: [01 / Packs] workflow-loop.003.md:99\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## Risk And Governing Targets\nsource: [01 / Risk And Governing Targets] workflow-loop.003.md:150-154\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## The Loop\nsource: [01 / The Loop] workflow-loop.003.md:160-177; 04 text: workflow-loop.002-notes.md:38\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- A closed counter is bare `05.NN`; `closed` is not a valid tail.\n\n## Return Placement\nsource: [01 / Return Placement] workflow-loop.003.md:181-183; 04 text: decisions.md:612\nwhy: 4.7\n\n- Where a return lives: on its counter; its findings are booked as 06 notes and 05 tasks on the pages they concern.\n\n## Review (filled)\nsource: [01 / Review (filled)] workflow-loop.003.md:199-202; 04 text: decisions.md:625-626,636 (F.1 S10); why: docs/spec/decisions.md:636\nwhy: Another view must test the artifacts against the criteria, with a deeper trial when inherited rules can change. E7 retains its open identity question.\n\n| Decision | Approved direction and scope | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| S10 | Require independent evidence review, proportionate trials, and a recorded N7 trial before promoting governing changes. | Another view must test the artifacts against the criteria, with a deeper trial when inherited rules can change. E7 retains its open identity question. |\n\n## Open While Gone\nsource: [01 / Open While Gone] workflow-loop.003.md:206-208; 04 text: decisions.md:625-626,637 (F.1 S11); why: docs/spec/decisions.md:637\nwhy: Competing claims and stale workers must not overwrite the record of an attempt.\n\n| Decision | Approved direction and scope | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| S11 | Record one valid claim, its owner, recovery terms, and lineage to any replacement counter. | Competing claims and stale workers must not overwrite the record of an attempt. |\n\n## Lead Pass\nsource: [01 / Lead Pass] workflow-loop.003.md:214-220; 04 text: decisions.md:625-626,632 (F.1 S06); why: docs/spec/decisions.md:632\nwhy: A cold builder can reproduce its starting context, and stale copies can be regenerated from the governing records.\n\n| Decision | Approved direction and scope | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| S06 | Derive a read-only builder handoff from exact source references, approved criteria, dependencies, and current pack state. | A cold builder can reproduce its starting context, and stale copies can be regenerated from the governing records. |\n\n## Promotion Manifest\nsource: [01 / Promotion Manifest] workflow-loop.003.md:224-226; 04 text: decisions.md:625-626,630 (F.1 S04); why: docs/spec/decisions.md:630\nwhy: Evidence that a pack passed establishes neither the whole MVP nor authority to change what is live.\n\n| Decision | Approved direction and scope | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| S04 | Record pack acceptance, MVP completion, and promotion separately. Allow routine promotion only within explicitly delegated N4 scope. | Evidence that a pack passed establishes neither the whole MVP nor authority to change what is live. |\n\n## Gravity\nsource: [01 / Gravity] workflow-loop.003.md:258-269, workflow-loop.003.md:280; 04 text: decisions.md:625-626,635 (F.1 S09); why: docs/spec/decisions.md:635\nwhy: Gravity must follow the work across different agents and expose repetition without counting unrelated work.\n\n| Decision | Approved direction and scope | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| S09 | Count passes per approved frame and track repeated blockers within it. | Gravity must follow the work across different agents and expose repetition without counting unrelated work. |\n\n## Pack Header\nsource: [02 / Pack Header] workflow-loop.003.md:103-116\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).\n\n## Work Card\nsource: [02 / Work Card] workflow-loop.003.md:120-146; 04 text: decisions.md:625-626,633 (F.1 S07); why: docs/spec/decisions.md:633\nwhy: This follows the SeqAddr card-key grammar and keeps card keys distinct from counters.\n\n| Decision | Approved direction and scope | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| S07 | Use lowercase `w01`, `w02`, and onward as the work-card keys. | This follows the SeqAddr card-key grammar and keeps card keys distinct from counters. |\n\n## Return Shape\nsource: [02 / Return Shape] workflow-loop.003.md:189-193; 04 text: decisions.md:625-626,628 (F.1 S02); why: docs/spec/decisions.md:628\nwhy: A reviewer must be able to test the claim, and the cost of verification should reflect the cost of error.\n\n| Decision | Approved direction and scope | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| S02 | Require inspectable evidence for returns in proportion to the risk of the work. | A reviewer must be able to test the claim, and the cost of verification should reflect the cost of error. |\n\n## Pack Lint\nsource: [02 / Pack Lint] workflow-loop.003.md:232-252; 04 text: decisions.md:625-626,634 (F.1 S08); why: docs/spec/decisions.md:634\nwhy: Dependencies and modification targets must resolve. A creation needs an available legal location.\n\n| Decision | Approved direction and scope | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| S08 | Validate dependencies, modifications, and creations according to their target intent. | Dependencies and modification targets must resolve. A creation needs an available legal location. |\n\n## Why Questions\nsource: [02 / Why Questions] workflow-loop.003.md:273-278\nwhy: 4.7\n\n`7` - no person-facing explanation of this card is written in the spec. A required absence: reported, not invented (axiom 7, decisions.md C3.2).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0057"}
{"n":36,"at":"2026-11-11T19:30:00Z","by":"prima-kernel-seed","op":"put","addr":"00.00.00.0057.06.01.draft","tail":"draft","reason":"this session's note, still being written (seqAddr.md:155 \"one counter per session\"). Changed in the second kernel pass (R4, R6, R9, R10) after TRIAL-K, so it needs a new trial (axiom 9).","body":"## Seed Session\n\nPrima seed session, 2026-11-11. Note fields are open (decisions.md C7), so this note carries none (`7`).\n\n- Source is `workflow-loop.003.md` (state: draft). `.002` (`workflow-loop.md`) is kept as lineage.\n- `7.8`: `workflow-loop.003-notes.md` is linked from `.003` lines 16, 295 and 301 but is not in docs/spec.\n- Open, copied (.003 section 10):\n\nThe Director approved S01-S12 and the following choices: delegated promotion *inside* approved N4 scope; risk-proportional evidence; risk-based N7 trials with **mandatory N7 for governing changes**; lowercase `w01` card keys. These are agreed directions, not proof of an implemented mechanism.\n\n- `7` **Risk and review:** target registry, calibrated levels, routine eligibility, any N7 threshold beyond governing changes, E7 reviewer identity, and reviewer reassignment after interruption.\n- `7` **Claim and return recovery:** lease/heartbeat duration, offline work, claim fencing, abandonment authority, durable return transition, and mixed outcome precedence.\n- `7` **Approval compatibility:** which kinds of N4 edits are material, exact revision-token representation, and how approval spans a changed source commit.\n- `7` **Promotion mechanics:** the MVP completion record, page-type restrictions, revision guards and mixed-store reconciliation.\n- `7` **Continuation allocation:** exact permissible free-band selection and subject set link migration in SeqAddr; do not invent a target.\n- `7` **Evidence policy:** minimal trusted evidence by work type and risk; verify source and claims rather than treating model text as proof.\n- `7` **Cross-book transactions:** when a pack writes reciprocal Face 08 links in separate physical books, require coordinated commit or a blocked/escalated operation.\n- `7` **Versioned governance:** seat F.2 through A2.6/A6.1 and align the kernel lint schema, plus the E8 and E9 dependencies named above. When revising the proposed read path, distinguish the lead's `pack` from Compute's handoff manifest.\n- `7` **External verification and gravity:** define the workspace attribution boundary and durable accounting for releases, blockers and continuations.\n- `9` **Trial:** hostile review of this draft and the scenarios in [`.003` notes](workflow-loop.003-notes.md#review-cases).\n- Merges from Director rulings (docs/spec/DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md: R1 door, R9 no face sentinels, R10 face 00 registry), one per change, copied text otherwise unchanged:\n  - R9 [01 / N4 Approval]: \"These are independent counted boards, not mirrored counters.\" -> \"These are independent counted faces, not mirrored counters.\"\n  - R9 [01 / The Loop]: \"The N5 `05.00` board shows each job counter `05.NN` as one Kanban card (A4.4). Its current tail determines its column. The counter keeps the same identity throughout. The lead, builder and reviewer complete their work on that card, and Compute records each handoff in its history before showing the card in the next column. The board derives its current view from that history.\" -> \"\"\n  - R9 [01 / The Loop]: \"`closed` is the board label for\" -> \"`closed` is the label for\"\n- R4 (DIRECTOR-RULINGS.md:15, plain characters only): replaced U+00A7 x7, U+2013 x16, U+201C x1, U+201D x1 with ASCII (dashes to \"-\", section sign to \"section \", curly quotes to straight).","proposal":"prima-kernel-seed:00.00.00.0057"}
